28cc" "q-79165/selection/f50abb079f365a16a9ff95d77296d9830a698ba4fbacfae3a775f393bfed6a3b" "q-79165/selection/17f98cbe9e41d0f32b9ea7200bf083df9dee609170abeef53cc5a90068e4b9c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79165" :question/id #uuid "1c32b602-47c9-5b39-83b7-5221df83596f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79165" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/c9e8fb87758eefb1facfea08112c5add0cc54d481e36e740d3cc623f05fa41fb.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/8a0491010e71d24ebac54b5b8d03b1ce45f7de90a0f21ff1ea2f0c2babf4ec77.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79165/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79165"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106299/selection/79ce4c0ffa390694dcda71be82d82e64c68ac5b04ba603f2b9f8d25747d96c69" :answer/id #uuid "31ba1573-e897-5353-8079-4fda64780606" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106299/selection/d5a97339d450307338b0d4e8f6e9e8a8bc4cdef9b3d035c8c87ef85658334cb9" :answer/id #uuid "be30d7bd-84ea-5bd7-b974-4492a9eed2f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106299/selection/bee397ead07dc280438bc6c2eab39ac8b325fb48416dac4e05e58d0af28afd03" :answer/id #uuid "682085f3-e6c6-5a9c-a986-a30dd806d7e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106299/selection/3cd6b19869008682ae2ea1b9545b338ba80a40711509b1d5cf90495571b33d54" :answer/id #uuid "d9178563-1530-5722-836e-3e683572f035" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106299/selection/d80f4bede42c6119be0c9ed33bd194a744e202d082effacf0a1e40762e00547a" :answer/id #uuid "47641c23-fe83-5c2e-9bce-80c458e600a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106299/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a8792f3-f6ae-5a7e-9d5a-96bb37db3af4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106299/selection/3cd6b19869008682ae2ea1b9545b338ba80a40711509b1d5cf90495571b33d54" :answer-field/choices ["q-106299/selection/79ce4c0ffa390694dcda71be82d82e64c68ac5b04ba603f2b9f8d25747d96c69" "q-106299/selection/d5a97339d450307338b0d4e8f6e9e8a8bc4cdef9b3d035c8c87ef85658334cb9" "q-106299/selection/bee397ead07dc280438bc6c2eab39ac8b325fb48416dac4e05e58d0af28afd03" "q-106299/selection/3cd6b19869008682ae2ea1b9545b338ba80a40711509b1d5cf90495571b33d54" "q-106299/selection/d80f4bede42c6119be0c9ed33bd194a744e202d082effacf0a1e40762e00547a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106299" :question/id #uuid "2ab0fea2-8c6c-59ec-a88b-21257097f620" :question/math-academy-id "q-106299" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(y+2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x+1)}^{2}+4{(y+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation of the circle is ${(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-106299/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106299"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-321988/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "8d84cb2b-0a25-5fc4-960e-170a3e2e4aa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321988/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d33d357-d449-5e18-b3a4-7a9139491d24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321988/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-321988/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321988/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "fef87a06-3a66-5dff-b54f-a5aecf6b338a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321988/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "99de5aba-8f34-55b2-8133-83921c54ab79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321988/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-321988/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321988/field-3/2ee71f97da76a36a5f98bee4ae2425715bbc4597486731aaed28becb23704cce" :answer/id #uuid "65f71130-8e9e-545f-863d-32655c304885" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321988/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "48d8da69-4abb-55e7-be09-b65c4129c777" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321988/field-3/2ee71f97da76a36a5f98bee4ae2425715bbc4597486731aaed28becb23704cce" :answer-field/choices ["q-321988/field-3/2ee71f97da76a36a5f98bee4ae2425715bbc4597486731aaed28becb23704cce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321988" :question/id #uuid "5ca6697a-5172-5e08-af3d-aff0cbcd8ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-321988" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(x+3) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(y-1) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-321988/field-1" "q-321988/field-2" "q-321988/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321988"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-6879"] :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =9(1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-321991/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "37511343-3a89-56ab-b839-1525b4ea9ee0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321991/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9afcc869-c49f-52fb-8874-10c9e350a7dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321991/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-321991/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321991/field-2/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer/id #uuid "18c47043-53d4-56e2-a8ab-5b6f50a2619d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321991/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "883b7e66-b945-5e6a-a4a9-dc30f84c3ec4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321991/field-2/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer-field/choices ["q-321991/field-2/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321991/field-3/ed46a7ab66f4e1f0492ab8ee0b3b7d22df7bff44011d5a9f713d6c9393035ddc" :answer/id #uuid "42d8e596-22bc-5a90-a3ab-fc5d023f9856" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321991/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b67d8a5-8504-5dfd-b964-be5b026dfbd0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321991/field-3/ed46a7ab66f4e1f0492ab8ee0b3b7d22df7bff44011d5a9f713d6c9393035ddc" :answer-field/choices ["q-321991/field-3/ed46a7ab66f4e1f0492ab8ee0b3b7d22df7bff44011d5a9f713d6c9393035ddc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321991" :question/id #uuid "381c6a8c-2101-5081-94d4-70ba5b197dd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-321991" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +13,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-13 & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-13)^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{(x+5)}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{(y-13)}^{2}}{49} & =1 \\\\ {(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+5)}^{2}+{(y-13)}^{2}=49.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-321991/field-1" "q-321991/field-2" "q-321991/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321991"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5374"] :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6779"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
